<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nguyen Hoang</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1525">1525</a>–<a href="/years/1613">1613</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nguyen Hoang was the first of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguyen-lords">Nguyen lords</a> of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. In 1558, to escape his rivals the Trinh lords, he had himself appointed governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuan-hoa">Thuan Hoa</a> in central Vietnam. He built up an autonomous southern realm, Dang Trong, that expanded at the expense of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/champa">Champa</a>, and his descendants later founded the Nguyen dynasty, Vietnam's last imperial house.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nguyen-hoang.jpg" alt="Nguyen Hoang" width="202" height="248" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nguyễn Hoàng Quý · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nguyen_hoang.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Nguyễn Hoàng was the second son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguyen-kim">Nguyễn Kim</a>. When his father was assassinated by a supporter of the Mạc, command of the Lê royalist army passed to his brother-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinh-kiem">Trịnh Kiểm</a>. His elder brother Nguyễn Uông died soon after, believed to have been poisoned by Trịnh Kiểm, and Nguyễn Hoàng asked to be sent to govern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuan-hoa">Thuận Hóa</a> in the south. The reason Trịnh Kiểm agreed is unclear; one anecdote holds that he hoped the Mạc garrison troops there would rid him of his brother-in-law.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuan-hoa">Thuận Hóa</a> had once been <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/champa">Champa</a> territory, ceded to Đại Việt by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cham">Cham</a> king Jaya Simhavarman III, and was then held by Mạc forces. Nguyễn Hoàng defeated their commander, the Duke of Lập, and took the province in 1558. In 1573 Emperor Lê Thế Tông gave him the title Grand Master (Thái phó), and he was later made Duke of Môn. He held court at Ái Tử from 1558 to 1570, at Trà Bát from 1570 to 1600 and at Dinh Cát, the present Huế, from 1600.</p><p>In 1592, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinh-tung">Trịnh Tùng</a> besieged the Mạc capital, the present <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a>, Nguyễn Hoàng sent troops and supplies, and his army helped destroy the remaining Mạc forces. For reasons that are not known, he refused to recognise the new emperor Lê Kinh Tông and in 1600 took the title Good Prince; his nephew Trịnh Tùng had received a similar title, Pacifying Prince, a year earlier.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Nguyễn Hoàng died on 20 July 1613 after ruling the south for 55 years. Of his ten sons most had died in battle or stayed in the north, and his sixth son Nguyễn Phúc Nguyên succeeded him. His descendant Nguyễn Ánh later became emperor of a united <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. He is still remembered in central and southern Vietnam, above all in Huế, where his 400th death anniversary was marked in 2013.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nguyễn Hoàng” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1558 <b>Nguyen Hoang</b>, son of the Le loyalist leader Nguyen Kim, had himself appointed governor of Thuan Hoa in central Vietnam to escape the Trinh lords.</blockquote><a href="/state/nguyen-lords">Nguyen lords</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thanh-hoa">Thanh Hóa</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. Died in Đàng Trong. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=20,105.5&amp;year=1582" aria-label="Thanh Hóa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Nguyen Hoang"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3847 5778h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3847 5778h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=20,105.5&amp;year=1582">What was here in 1582? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
